<Base title="How I work">
  <h1>How I work</h1>
  <p class="lede">The site is the output of a workflow, not a set of one-off notebooks. This page describes the workflow so the projects can be judged on it, including what goes wrong.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Principles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Raw data is immutable.</strong> API responses are archived as they arrived; bugs are fixed in transforms, never by rewriting history.</li>
      <li><strong>Results are not typed into prose.</strong> Each project page is templated from a facts file the pipeline writes, and a test scans the page sources for hand-typed data numbers. Definitional constants (a 10-cent spread threshold, a 0.5% tolerance) are the allowed exception and are listed in the test.</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluation is written before results exist.</strong> The scoring rules for the two live prediction-market projects are committed in one file whose git hash is printed on the pages.</li>
      <li><strong>One chart system.</strong> A fixed palette validated for colour-vision deficiency on both themes, titles that state the finding and are themselves templated from the data, sources and as-of stamps on every chart.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tooling</h2>
    <p>Python with Polars for pipelines, pandera for data contracts, pytest for parsers and page guardrails, GitHub Actions for scheduling, Astro and Vega-Lite for the site, GitHub Pages for hosting. All free, all public.</p>

    <h2>Working with Claude Code</h2>
    <p>Claude Code writes most of the code and all of the boilerplate. The repo carries a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with the conventions above and four project skills: <code>portfolio-project-scaffold</code> (create a project with the standard layout), <code>chart-review</code> (check a spec against the chart rules), <code>data-audit</code> (freshness, ranges, duplicates, reconciliation) and <code>writeup</code> (draft Method, Evaluation and Build notes with every number routed through facts). I decide the questions, the market sets, the metric definitions and the evaluation plans, and I review every chart and every paragraph before it ships.</p>

    <h2>Acceptance reviews</h2>
    <p>After each milestone, independent read-only reviewer agents audit the repo against the original written plan: one for the pipelines and data layer, one for the site and pages. They report PASS / PARTIAL / FAIL per checklist item with file-and-line evidence. Their findings drive the next round of fixes and are summarised in the changelog. The first two reviews produced 47 findings; the ones that changed the site are in the failure log below.</p>

    <h2>Quality gates</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Schema and range checks on every pipeline output before it is written; a failing check blocks the write.</li>
      <li>Unit tests on the parsers and the differencing rules; a page-source guardrail against hand-typed numbers; both run in CI before every deploy.</li>
      <li>Pipelines commit their own data, and the site rebuilds after every data run, so the Actions history is the audit trail and the "data as of" stamps are real.</li>
      <li>A data-quality table on every project page shows the checks that ran for that build.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Failure log</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kalshi universe empty.</strong> The first midterms snapshot captured almost no state races: Kalshi closes 2026 election markets on 2027-11-03, a year after election day, and the close-time filter ended in early 2027. Fix: widen the window; document the reason in the data model.</li>
      <li><strong>Deploy failed twice on <code>npm ci</code>.</strong> Lockfiles written on macOS omit Linux-only optional bindings, so the Linux runner rejected them. Fix: <code>npm install</code> in CI, documented in the workflow.</li>
      <li><strong>Data refreshes did not redeploy the site.</strong> Pushes made with the Actions token do not trigger push workflows, so the live "data as of" stamps lagged the repo. Caught by the first acceptance review. Fix: deploy on <code>workflow_run</code> after every data job.</li>
      <li><strong>A chart title said something the data did not.</strong> The stat-arb cost chart claimed costs decided the sign; at zero cost every static model was already negative. Caught by the second acceptance review. Fix: the title is now computed from the cost curve, and titles are covered by the numbers guardrail.</li>
      <li><strong>A truncated axis made a small gain look large.</strong> The Financial LLM summary bars started at 0.70; the bars overflowed the plot and exaggerated a 0.03 gain. Fix: zero baseline with value labels.</li>
      <li><strong>Settled markets vanish.</strong> Both platforms drop settled markets from their open listings, so the FOMC scorecard could never fill. Fix: every full run now records settlements to an append-only store.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
